paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:4:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection framework whose headline method is score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling: a simple arithmetic average of a Direct VLM score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} ($1{,}418$ test items, 12 domains) the ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). On GPT-5.4 it reaches $0.864$, edging the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline by $+0.9$ pp while using no external expert model. We localise the mechanism to \emph{score-rank granularity}: Direct emits $11$ unique scores on the test set, the agent emits $49$; averaging breaks ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with the agent's finer rank information. Controlled rank-preserving transformations confirm this: $0\%$ middle-mass with $49$ unique values still delivers $+4.68$ pp, whereas $0\%$ middle-mass with only $2$ unique values delivers $+2.31$ pp.
paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:6:We also introduced a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that instantiates a ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, a refutation tool checks each against the reference pool, surviving features count as anomaly evidence. v8 ensembled with Direct gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (positive one-sided bootstrap probability $0.962$; 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$ touches zero). v8 is positioned as an interpretable variant, not the main method: it produces per-item traces (candidate list, refutation verdict per tool call, remaining candidates) that a reviewer can audit, at the cost of $3.3$ pp relative to the plain $0.5$-average v6 ensemble. The smaller v8 gain is consistent with its coarser rank grid, closing the loop between deliberation-protocol design and the rank-granularity mechanism.
paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:11:The v6 ensemble's macro-AUROC gain over SubspaceAD fusion is positive only on GPT-5.4 ($+0.9$ pp) and is tied on Qwen3.5 ($\pm 0$ pp). The refutation agent's Qwen3.5 test gain is positive-bootstrap but its CI touches zero; its stored test JSON was produced before a minor schema fix (corrected code is released; re-run not executed). GPT-5.4 v8 test at scale repeatedly rate-limits our proprietary API gateway, so GPT-5.4 numbers for v8 are dev-only ($n{=}480$). The v8 ensemble claim therefore generalises across backbones only at dev-split resolution, not test. The agent+Direct recipe matches the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline on Qwen3.5 rather than strictly outperforming it; we report it as a VLM-only alternative that avoids a domain-specialised expert.
paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:14:Three directions follow from the rank-granularity mechanism: (i) deliberation protocols designed for a fine rank grid (e.g.\ requiring the VLM to output continuous refutation-strength scores rather than verdict categories) should improve v8's ensemble gain; (ii) a principled calibration-split ensemble weight selector per backbone, rather than our dev-frozen $w$; (iii) extension of the refutation protocol to operate on multiple candidate-feature lists produced from different prompt seeds, combining internal hypothesis diversity with our cross-system ensemble.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:5:\textbf{Mechanism.} Controlled rank-preserving ablations localise the gain to \emph{score-rank granularity}, not to middle-zone mass. Direct emits only 11 unique score values on our test set whereas the v6 ReAct agent emits 49; the agent's finer rank grid breaks ties inside Direct's bimodal clusters when the two scores are averaged. A rank-preserving transformation that keeps the agent's 49 unique scores but pushes them all outside $[0.2, 0.8]$ preserves the ensemble gain ($+4.68$ pp) despite $0\%$ middle-zone mass; a rank-collapsing binarisation (two unique values) drops the gain to $+2.31$ pp; a random middle-mass noise partner (uniform in $[0.2, 0.8]$) gives $-3.4$ pp. Middle-zone mass is therefore correlated with ensemble gain only because it correlates with rank granularity; when we decouple them, granularity is what matters. The deeper ingredients are: (i) the agent's score has non-trivial standalone signal (random score with no signal hurts the ensemble); (ii) the agent has a distinct rank grid with many unique values; (iii) the agent's errors are complementary to the VLM's.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:7:\textbf{Secondary contribution.} We introduce a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that implements an explicit ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, each is targeted by a refutation tool, surviving candidates yield anomaly evidence. v8 is interpretable---a reviewer can audit exactly which features survived and why---and gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (CI $[-0.001,+0.027]$) when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain relative to v6 is explained by the same rank-granularity mechanism: v8's explicit refutation protocol collapses scores to a small number of unique values ($0.05, 0.85$) which preserves correct rank on refuted vs surviving features but destroys rank granularity within each group.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:9:\textbf{Takeaway.} Training-free VAD benefits from \emph{score-diverse} VLM-agent ensembling; the gain is explained by complementary rank structure, not by stronger standalone agents. We release the v6/v8 agent implementations, per-tool single-tool audit framework (13 tools, dev n=480), and full paired-bootstrap analysis artifacts.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:8:We evaluate on \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, spanning twelve domain codes grouped into 11 reported families: D1 MVTec-AD industrial, D2 GoodsAD retail, D3 SDNET2018 concrete infrastructure (dev only), D4 DermaMNIST dermoscopy, D5 BraTS2021 brain MRI, D5b BMAD brain MRI, D5c BMAD liver CT, D5d HyperKvasir GI endoscopy variant, D6 LEVIR-CD+ remote-sensing change, D7 HyperKvasir GI endoscopy, D8 RA21+BDD road, D9 MVTec-LOCO logical, D10 VisA industrial. Each domain contributes 20 calibration / 40 dev / 120 test items (D8: 98 test). All items are reference-based, paired with 1--10 normal references. Total test items $n{=}1418$ (fusion strategies drop D8 items lacking SubspaceAD support to $n{=}1298$ and fall back to direct on the rest). The full per-domain specification is in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:11:Three VLMs spanning proprietary--open: \textbf{GPT-5.4} (proprietary frontier), \textbf{SeedVL} (\texttt{doubao-seed-2-0-lite}, proprietary non-frontier), and \textbf{Qwen3.5-VL-27B} (open-weight, served locally via vLLM, thinking disabled). Temperature $0$, identical prompts.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:14:(i) \textbf{DINOv2-PatchNN}: patch-$k$-NN expert alone. (ii) \textbf{SubspaceAD}: PCA subspace expert alone~\citep{subspacead2026}. (iii) \textbf{Direct VLM} (v0): one VLM call with task-anchored descriptor. (iv) \textbf{Debate}: proposer--advocate, 2 VLM calls. (v) \textbf{Interpret}: AnomalyClaw v1 three-route router, $\sim 1.3$ VLM calls. (vi) \textbf{Fusion}: $s = 0.8\,s_0 + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$. (vii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Descriptor Router}: per-domain rule $S(d)$ derived from the family taxonomy (\S\ref{ssec:router}; simulated by score look-up on the existing per-strategy outputs). (viii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Calibration Router}: per-domain argmax strategy chosen from 20 calibration items per domain (simulated by score look-up). (ix) \textbf{AnomalyClaw v3 (real autonomous agent)}: end-to-end agent that runs the autonomous-planner VLM call per domain and executes the chosen tool/expert/strategy plan per item with the online expert override. (x) \textbf{Oracle}: per-domain argmax computed on test labels (upper bound; cheats).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:17:Per-domain AUROC; macro-averaged across the 12 domain codes present in test. All thresholds and router assignments frozen from calibration. VLM anomaly scores extracted via structured JSON output (schema in Appendix~\ref{app:scoring}); $s = c$ if label is ``anomalous'' else $1 - c$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs via stratified paired bootstrap (per-domain stratification, $1{,}000$ resamples).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:24:\caption{\textbf{Main results: per-domain AUROC across three VLM backbones} (test split, $n{=}1{,}418$). For each backbone we report the descriptor-only Direct VLM and our \emph{agent+Direct ensemble} ($s = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, weights frozen on dev). Agent choice per backbone: v6.5 on Qwen3.5/SeedVL, v6.6 self-ensemble on GPT-5.4 (selected on dev). \textbf{Macro} row at the bottom; $\Delta$ is ensemble macro minus Direct macro. 95\% paired bootstrap CIs on macro $\Delta$ are reported under the table; all three backbones show $P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!\geq\!0.999$.}
