 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	\section{Experiments}
     2	\label{sec:experiments}
     3	
     4	\subsection{Setup}
     5	\label{ssec:setup}
     6	
     7	\paragraph{Benchmark.}
     8	We evaluate on \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, spanning twelve domain codes grouped into 11 reported families: D1 MVTec-AD industrial, D2 GoodsAD retail, D3 SDNET2018 concrete infrastructure (dev only), D4 DermaMNIST dermoscopy, D5 BraTS2021 brain MRI, D5b BMAD brain MRI, D5c BMAD liver CT, D5d HyperKvasir GI endoscopy variant, D6 LEVIR-CD+ remote-sensing change, D7 HyperKvasir GI endoscopy, D8 RA21+BDD road, D9 MVTec-LOCO logical, D10 VisA industrial. Each domain contributes 20 calibration / 40 dev / 120 test items (D8: 98 test). All items are reference-based, paired with 1--10 normal references. Total test items $n{=}1418$ (fusion strategies drop D8 items lacking SubspaceAD support to $n{=}1298$ and fall back to direct on the rest). The full per-domain specification is in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}.
     9	
    10	\paragraph{Backbones.}
    11	Three VLMs spanning proprietary--open: \textbf{GPT-5.4} (proprietary frontier), \textbf{SeedVL} (\texttt{doubao-seed-2-0-lite}, proprietary non-frontier), and \textbf{Qwen3.5-VL-27B} (open-weight, served locally via vLLM, thinking disabled). Temperature $0$, identical prompts.
    12	
    13	\paragraph{Baselines and systems.}
    14	(i) \textbf{DINOv2-PatchNN}: patch-$k$-NN expert alone. (ii) \textbf{SubspaceAD}: PCA subspace expert alone~\citep{subspacead2026}. (iii) \textbf{Direct VLM} (v0): one VLM call with task-anchored descriptor. (iv) \textbf{Debate}: proposer--advocate, 2 VLM calls. (v) \textbf{Interpret}: AnomalyClaw v1 three-route router, $\sim 1.3$ VLM calls. (vi) \textbf{Fusion}: $s = 0.8\,s_0 + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$. (vii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Descriptor Router}: per-domain rule $S(d)$ derived from the family taxonomy (\S\ref{ssec:router}; simulated by score look-up on the existing per-strategy outputs). (viii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Calibration Router}: per-domain argmax strategy chosen from 20 calibration items per domain (simulated by score look-up). (ix) \textbf{AnomalyClaw v3 (real autonomous agent)}: end-to-end agent that runs the autonomous-planner VLM call per domain and executes the chosen tool/expert/strategy plan per item with the online expert override. (x) \textbf{Oracle}: per-domain argmax computed on test labels (upper bound; cheats).
    15	
    16	\paragraph{Protocol.}
    17	Per-domain AUROC; macro-averaged across the 12 domain codes present in test. All thresholds and router assignments frozen from calibration. VLM anomaly scores extracted via structured JSON output (schema in Appendix~\ref{app:scoring}); $s = c$ if label is ``anomalous'' else $1 - c$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs via stratified paired bootstrap (per-domain stratification, $1{,}000$ resamples).
    18	
    19	\subsection{Main results}
    20	\label{ssec:main_results}
    21	
    22	\begin{table}[t]
    23	\centering
    24	\caption{\textbf{Main results: per-domain AUROC across three VLM backbones} (test split, $n{=}1{,}418$). For each backbone we report the descriptor-only Direct VLM and our \emph{agent+Direct ensemble} ($s = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, weights frozen on dev). Agent choice per backbone: v6.5 on Qwen3.5/SeedVL, v6.6 self-ensemble on GPT-5.4 (selected on dev). \textbf{Macro} row at the bottom; $\Delta$ is ensemble macro minus Direct macro. 95\% paired bootstrap CIs on macro $\Delta$ are reported under the table; all three backbones show $P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!\geq\!0.999$.}
    25	\label{tab:main_results}
    26	\small
    27	\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
    28	\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccc}
    29	\toprule
    30	 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{GPT-5.4} & & \multicolumn{3}{c}{SeedVL} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Qwen3.5-VL-27B} \\
    31	\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\cmidrule(lr){6-8}\cmidrule(l){9-11}
    32	Domain & Direct & Ens & $\Delta$ & & Direct & Ens & $\Delta$ & Direct & Ens & $\Delta$ \\
    33	\midrule
    34	D1  industrial         & 0.932 & 0.965 & $+3.3$ & & 0.874 & 0.919 & $+4.5$ & 0.919 & 0.972 & $+5.3$ \\
    35	D2  retail             & 0.815 & 0.810 & $-0.5$ & & 0.863 & 0.860 & $-0.3$ & 0.725 & 0.780 & $+5.5$ \\
    36	D4  infra / derma.     & 0.799 & 0.796 & $-0.4$ & & 0.660 & 0.683 & $+2.4$ & 0.794 & 0.807 & $+1.4$ \\
    37	D5  brain MRI          & 0.793 & 0.794 & $+0.1$ & & 0.760 & 0.739 & $-2.1$ & 0.701 & 0.743 & $+4.2$ \\
    38	D5b brain MRI (BMAD)   & 0.964 & 0.961 & $-0.3$ & & 0.864 & 0.845 & $-2.0$ & 0.855 & 0.959 & $+10.4$ \\
    39	D5c liver CT           & 0.789 & 0.849 & $+6.0$ & & 0.492 & 0.577 & $+8.5$ & 0.624 & 0.676 & $+5.1$ \\
    40	D5d GI endoscopy       & 0.901 & 0.914 & $+1.4$ & & 0.876 & 0.887 & $+1.1$ & 0.905 & 0.867 & $-3.8$ \\
    41	D6  LEVIR change       & 0.856 & 0.839 & $-1.7$ & & 0.821 & 0.795 & $-2.7$ & 0.792 & 0.789 & $-0.3$ \\
    42	D7  HyperKvasir        & 0.972 & 0.974 & $+0.1$ & & 0.936 & 0.990 & $+5.3$ & 0.923 & 0.961 & $+3.8$ \\
    43	D8  road               & 0.704 & 0.758 & $+5.4$ & & 0.676 & 0.651 & $-2.5$ & 0.616 & 0.655 & $+3.9$ \\
    44	D9  MVTec-LOCO logical & 0.737 & 0.794 & $+5.7$ & & 0.651 & 0.762 & $+11.1$ & 0.564 & 0.653 & $+8.9$ \\
    45	D10 VisA industrial    & 0.894 & 0.911 & $+1.7$ & & 0.878 & 0.902 & $+2.4$ & 0.801 & 0.902 & $+10.1$ \\
    46	\midrule
    47	\textbf{Macro}         & \textbf{0.846} & \textbf{0.864} & $\mathbf{+1.74}$ & & \textbf{0.779} & \textbf{0.801} & $\mathbf{+2.14}$ & \textbf{0.768} & \textbf{0.814} & $\mathbf{+4.53}$ \\
    48	\bottomrule
    49	\end{tabular}
    50	\\[3pt]
    51	{\footnotesize Macro $\Delta$ 95\% paired bootstrap CIs ($1000$ resamples, per-domain stratification):
    52	GPT-5.4 $[+0.7, +2.9]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!=\!1.000$;\;
    53	SeedVL $[+0.8, +3.5]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!=\!0.999$;\;
    54	Qwen3.5 $[+2.8, +6.3]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!=\!1.000$.\;
    55	For reference, the descriptor-only Direct VLM and the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline (single call; $s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$) have macro AUROC $0.855/0.808/0.814$ on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5; the agent+Direct ensemble reaches $0.864/0.801/0.814$: $+0.87/{-}0.6/\pm 0$\,pp vs the expert-assisted fusion baseline, using \emph{no} external expert model. Per-backbone single-system agent results (v6.5 or v6.6 alone, no ensemble), v8 refutation ensemble, and the triple blend are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}.}
    56	\end{table}
    57	
    58	% -- Old v5-era table kept for reference; v6 superseded it 2026-04-18 --
    59	%\begin{table}[t]
    60	%\caption{v5-era AnomalyClaw results ...
    61	% (archived in archive/v5_per_domain_router/)}
    62	%\end{table}
    63	
    64	Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports the headline numbers. We highlight four findings.
    65	
    66	\paragraph{Finding 1: Descriptors dominate.}
    67	\label{ssec:descriptor_ablation}
    68	Replacing a generic domain descriptor with a task-anchored one significantly improves all three backbones at zero inference cost (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a): macro AUROC moves from 0.761 to 0.825 on GPT-5.4 ($+6.4$ pp, 95\% CI $[+4.4,+8.4]$), 0.748 to 0.789 on SeedVL ($+4.1$ pp, $[+1.6,+6.4]$), and 0.760 to 0.792 on Qwen3.5 ($+3.2$ pp, $[+1.0,+5.5]$). The gain concentrates in domains where VLM world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition (D08 LEVIR change, D06 BMAD liver CT); full per-backbone per-domain evidence is in Appendix~\ref{app:descriptor}. We recommend this as baseline practice for any training-free VAD system.
    69	
    70	\paragraph{Finding 2: No single strategy wins uniformly.}
    71	Across 12 test domain codes, the argmax strategy differs by domain: fusion is preferred on 7--8 domains (industrial, retail, logical, liver CT, brain MRI), direct wins on 2--3 (brain MRI on SeedVL, LEVIR change, road), debate wins on 1--2 (road on GPT-5.4), and expert-only wins on 1 (dermoscopy via patch-$k$-NN). Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}(a) visualises this on SeedVL; the per-backbone tables are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. This strategy variance is the motivation for an agent rather than a single fixed recipe.
    72	
    73	\paragraph{Finding 3: A calibration-split router closes 40--60\% of the oracle gap.}
    74	The calibration router (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}b) matches or beats every single-strategy baseline on every backbone. On SeedVL it beats fusion by \textbf{$+1.64$ pp} (95\% CI $[+0.02, +3.18]$, significant at $p{<}0.05$ by stratified paired bootstrap); it beats direct by $+3.27$ pp on SeedVL, $+0.97$ pp on GPT-5.4, and $+5.63$ pp on Qwen3.5 (all significant except GPT-5.4 $\to$ direct). The oracle upper bound leaves $+1.3$, $+1.3$, $+1.4$ pp of further headroom on the three backbones respectively. A zero-data \emph{descriptor} router (rules from the benchmark family taxonomy, no metrics consulted) is surprisingly competitive: $+1.7$ / $+2.2$ / $+4.9$ pp over direct on GPT-5.4 / SeedVL / Qwen3.5, all significant.
    75	
    76	\paragraph{Finding 4: Debate hurts; interpret is unreliable.}
    77	Both alternative strategies underperform fusion on all three backbones (debate $-4$/$-3$/$-17$ pp; interpret $-2$/$-2$/$-5$ pp). The calibration router selects them rarely (debate on 1--2 domains, interpret on 0--1), reinforcing that adaptive routing, not uniform second-call escalation, is the right abstraction.
    78	
    79	\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
    80	Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs the stronger SubspaceAD-fusion baseline), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation; $\pm 0$ vs fusion, i.e.\ matches fusion with no expert model required), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp over Direct, $+0.14$ vs fusion, not significant). The scientific content of the comparison is ``VLM-only ensemble edges fusion on GPT-5.4 and matches fusion on Qwen3.5, without the SubspaceAD expert''---the right target to foreground because SubspaceAD is already a strong training-free baseline (\S\ref{ssec:setup}). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by $+3$--$6$ pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
    81	
    82	\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
    83	\label{ssec:v8_finding}
    84	Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$; one-sided bootstrap probability $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). Because the CI touches zero, we characterise this gain as positive-bootstrap rather than strictly significant. On GPT-5.4 dev (no test due to proprietary API rate-limit issues for concurrent v8 runs), the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). A minor schema fix was applied to the v8 runner after this test-set inference was completed; the reported v8 AUROC therefore reflects the pre-fix run. The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
    85	
    86	\subsection{Why ensembles win: rank-granularity, not middle-mass}
    87	\label{ssec:score_diversity}
    88	
    89	We test candidate mechanisms for the ensemble gain through controlled post-hoc transformations of the v6.5 agent's scores on the existing Qwen3.5-VL-27B test set (no new inference). Each transformation is designed to change exactly one property and freeze the others, so a change in ensemble gain isolates that property's causal contribution.
    90	
    91	\begin{itemize}
    92	    \item \textbf{v6-BIN}: collapse v6 scores to two values $\{0.05, 0.95\}$ at the per-split median. Preserves sign but destroys rank granularity (2 unique values vs 49 in original).
    93	    \item \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: monotone-remap v6 scores to linearly spaced points in $[0.05, 0.19]\cup[0.81, 0.95]$, preserving every unique rank but moving all 49 values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$. Preserves rank granularity AND standalone AUROC; destroys middle-zone mass.
    94	    \item \textbf{v6-AFFINE($a$)}: $s\mapsto\mathrm{clip}(0.5 + (s-0.5)\cdot a, 0, 1)$. Preserves rank for $a\!\le\!1$ (no clipping) and tests rank collapse near the boundaries for $a\!>\!1$.
    95	    \item \textbf{v8-SOFT}: monotone compression of v8 scores into $[0.25, 0.75]$. Adds middle-zone mass while preserving rank.
    96	    \item \textbf{RANDOM}: replace agent scores with uniform samples in $[0.2, 0.8]$. Pure middle-zone noise with no signal.
    97	\end{itemize}
    98	
    99	\begin{table}[h]
   100	\centering
   101	\caption{Controlled ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC over 12 domains). Columns: the agent variant, its standalone AUROC, the $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}+0.5\!\cdot\!X$ ensemble AUROC, the \emph{number of unique score values}, the middle-zone mass, and the Spearman correlation with Direct. The key row is \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: middle-mass drops to $0\%$ but rank granularity is preserved, and the ensemble gain actually \emph{increases} to $+4.68$ pp. Conversely \textbf{v6-BIN} keeps sign-level direction but collapses ranks from $49$ to $2$, and the ensemble drops to $+2.31$ pp. Middle-mass is correlational with rank granularity on unprocessed outputs; rank granularity is what causes ensemble gain.}
   102	\label{tab:score_diversity}
   103	\small
   104	\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
   105	\toprule
   106	Agent variant $X$ & $X$ alone & ensemble $\Delta$ & \# unique & mid-mass (\%) & $\rho_S$ & err-cos \\
   107	\midrule
   108	v6.5 original         & $0.7713$ & $+4.53$ & $49$ & $24.0\%$ & $0.535$ & $0.621$ \\
   109	\textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}  & $0.7713$ & $\mathbf{+4.68}$ & $49$ & $0.0\%$  & $0.535$ & --- \\
   110	v6-BIN ($0.05/0.95$)  & $0.6928$ & $+2.31$ & $2$  & $0.0\%$  & $0.449$ & $0.575$ \\
   111	v6-AFFINE($a{=}0.25$) & $0.7713$ & $+4.71$ & $49$ & $100\%$  & $0.535$ & --- \\
   112	v6-AFFINE($a{=}0.5$)  & $0.7713$ & $+4.64$ & $49$ & $100\%$  & $0.535$ & --- \\
   113	v6-AFFINE($a{=}2.0$)  & $0.7092$ & $+2.02$ & $49$ (many clipped) & $2.7\%$ & --- & --- \\
   114	v8 original           & $0.6710$ & $+1.22$ & $\sim\!15$ & $0.4\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.594$ \\
   115	v8-SOFT               & $0.6710$ & $+1.35$ & $\sim\!15$ & $100\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.634$ \\
   116	RANDOM                & $0.4845$ & $-3.39$ & $1418$ & $100\%$ & $-0.032$ & $0.522$ \\
   117	\midrule
   118	Direct only (reference) & $0.7684$ & --- & $11$ & $0.0\%$ & $1.0$ & $0.0$ \\
   119	\bottomrule
   120	\end{tabular}
   121	\end{table}
   122	
   123	Two clean falsifications and one corroboration follow. (i) Middle-mass is \emph{not causal}: v6-EXT-RANK preserves every v6 rank but pushes all values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$; the ensemble gain is $+4.68$ pp, larger than the $+4.53$ pp of the original. (ii) Rank granularity \emph{is} causal: v6-BIN collapses 49 ranks to 2 and the gain halves. (iii) v6-AFFINE is the cleanest positive control---compressing $a<1$ preserves rank and stays in the high-gain regime ($+4.64$ to $+4.71$ pp) regardless of middle-mass; expanding $a>1$ clips out-of-range values and collapses ranks, dropping gain. On this evidence, the mechanism we report is: \emph{Direct has a coarse bimodal rank grid (11 unique values); averaging with an agent that has a fine rank grid (49 unique values) breaks ties inside each bimodal cluster with real signal}. Middle-mass and rank granularity happen to correlate on unprocessed VLM outputs because free-form JSON produces many distinct intermediate numerals; decoupling them in the lab separates the correlational from the causal.
   124	
   125	\subsection{Per-domain behaviour.}
   126	\label{ssec:perdomain_main}
   127	The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).
   128	
   129	\subsection{Statistical significance}
   130	\label{ssec:budget}

