     4	\subsection{A unified task-aware agent for MMAD}
     5	\label{ssec:mmad_fulltype}
     6	
     7	The cross-benchmark MMAD evaluation in \S\ref{ssec:mmad} used only the
     8	Anomaly-Detection subset (Yes/No). MMAD additionally contains eight
     9	$4$-way multiple-choice question types that probe \emph{defect
    10	classification} (\num{4688} questions), \emph{defect localization}
    11	(\num{4878}), \emph{defect description} (\num{4710}), \emph{defect
    12	analysis} (\num{4782}), and four \emph{object}-understanding types
    13	(\num{12315} combined). We extend our agent to a \emph{unified
    14	task-aware} output head that accepts an optional question and a set of
    15	multiple-choice options and produces, in a single forward pass, (i) a
    16	continuous anomaly score (for ensembling on AD), (ii) a letter answer
    17	for MCQ, and (iii) free-text when no options are present. The mode is
    18	inferred from the input inside the first cognitive turn---no separate
    19	routing call---so the same agent that produces anomaly scores on
    20	CrossDomainVAD also answers MCQ items on MMAD.
    21	
    22	\paragraph{Setup.}
    23	We fit per-class decision thresholds for the AD subset on a
    24	stratified $n{=}\TODO_DEV$-image \emph{dev} sample (all 38 product
    25	classes, seed $= 42$), then freeze and evaluate on a held-out
    26	$n{=}\TODO_TEST$-image stratified \emph{test} sample (different seed).
    27	Ensembling is applied only to AD questions; for the remaining eight
    28	MCQ types we compare the unified agent's letter prediction against
    29	the same Direct~VLM baseline with a strictly comparable MCQ-aware
    30	prompt. Backbone: Qwen3.5-VL-27B, temperature~$0$, same $4$ normal
    31	reference templates as \S\ref{ssec:mmad}.
    32	
