   158	
   159	Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} summarises the stratified paired bootstrap for the headline comparisons. The calibration router strictly beats every single-strategy baseline on SeedVL (including fusion, significant); it ties fusion on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 (fusion is the dominant single strategy on those backbones). Against the weaker \emph{direct} baseline that prior agentic-VAD work usually compares to, all three routers---descriptor, calibration, and oracle---show significant gains.
   160	
   161	\subsection{Cross-benchmark generalisation: MMAD}
   162	\label{ssec:mmad}
   163	
   164	We re-evaluated our Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent+Direct ensemble on \textbf{MMAD}~\citep{jiang2024mmad}, a $39{,}670$-question industrial-inspection MCQ benchmark whose Anomaly-Detection subset contains $8{,}297$ Yes/No binary-judgment questions over MVTec-AD, MVTec-LOCO, VisA, and GoodsAD. We report on a stratified $989$-item sample balanced across all $38$ product classes (5--$\sim$50 items per class, seed$=42$). Each query uses the first four \texttt{random\_templates} from \texttt{mmad.json} as normal references (matching MMAD's few-shot$=4$ setup); the Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent is the same unmodified \texttt{agent\_v6.py} used in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, and the ensemble is the same $0.5$-average recipe with no re-tuning.
   165	
   166	\begin{table}[h]
   167	\centering
   168	\caption{\textbf{MMAD cross-benchmark transfer} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B, stratified $n{=}989$ across $38$ classes, Anomaly-Detection subset). We report both the MMAD MCQ accuracy (A=Yes/B=No answer from score${>}0.5$) and the AUROC that is natively comparable to Table~\ref{tab:main_results}. AUROC gain transfers ($+2.31$ pp pooled, $95\%$ CI $[+0.8,+3.9]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.996$); MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain, consistent with the rank-granularity mechanism of \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging two scores changes rank ordering but rarely flips a score's sign relative to $0.5$.}
   169	\label{tab:mmad}
   170	\small
   171	\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
   172	\toprule
   173	 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MCQ Accuracy (\%)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Score AUROC} \\
   174	\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\cmidrule(l){5-7}
   175	Subset (\# items)         & Direct & Agent & Ens. & Direct & Agent & Ens. \\
   176	\midrule
   177	DS-MVTec (197)            & 82.2 & 81.2 & 83.2 & 0.914 & 0.890 & 0.930 \\
   178	MVTec-LOCO (187)          & 66.8 & 64.2 & 66.8 & 0.849 & 0.789 & 0.861 \\
   179	VisA (253)                & 67.6 & 70.0 & 67.2 & 0.851 & 0.828 & 0.883 \\
   180	GoodsAD (347)             & 56.8 & 53.3 & 54.2 & --- & --- & --- \\
   181	MVTec-AD (5)              & 40.0 & 40.0 & 40.0 & --- & --- & --- \\
   182	\midrule
   183	\textbf{Pooled} (989)     & \textbf{66.4} & 65.1 & 65.6 & \textbf{0.708} & 0.670 & \textbf{0.731} \\
   184	$\Delta$ Ens $-$ Direct   & --- & --- & $-0.8$ & --- & --- & $\mathbf{+0.023}$ \\
   185	\bottomrule
   186	\end{tabular}
   187	\\[3pt]
   188	{\footnotesize GoodsAD and MVTec-AD do not report AUROC because the stratified $989$-item sample has only one label class present for these subsets (GoodsAD: all 347 items label$=$1 because the subset has no dedicated ``good'' folder; MVTec-AD: $n{=}5$ sample is too small). The subset-level accuracy is reported without AUROC. MCQ Accuracy uses the mapping score${>}0.5 \rightarrow$A, else B.}
   189	\end{table}
   190	
   191	Two findings follow. First, the AUROC gain transfers: pooled AUROC moves from $0.708$ (Direct) to $0.731$ (agent$+$Direct ensemble), $+2.31$ pp with $95\%$ CI $[+0.8, +3.9]$ on a paired bootstrap---the same direction and comparable magnitude as Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s Qwen3.5 test gain. Second, MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp vs Direct). This is consistent with \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging scores changes the \emph{rank order} between positive and negative items (helps AUROC) but rarely crosses the decision threshold at $0.5$ (does not help accuracy). A method that needs to improve threshold-based accuracy would need, in addition, a calibrated threshold or a decision rule that exploits the agent's disagreement with Direct near $0.5$; we leave that to future work. The important conclusion is that the ensemble mechanism is benchmark-agnostic at the \emph{ranking} level: on a completely independent benchmark, with different reference-template construction and class taxonomy, it delivers the same AUROC improvement at no inference-time re-tuning.
   192	
   193	\paragraph{Hyperparameter protocol and sensitivity.}
   194	All hyperparameters are frozen from the 20-item-per-domain calibration split and never re-tuned on dev/test: fusion weight $w{=}0.2$ (selected on GPT-5.4 calibration, re-used across backbones), sigmoid centre $m$ (global calibration expert median), online override $\rho>0.8$, descriptor-router rules (no metrics consulted), and calibration-router argmax. Leave-one-domain-out: the calibration-router--fusion gap is positive on 10/12 splits for SeedVL ($-0.8/+2.5/0.79$ pp min/max/std) and 9/12 for Qwen3.5; full table Appendix~\ref{app:loo}.
   195	
   196	\subsection{Negative results: why simpler agents fail}
   197	\label{ssec:ablation}
   198	
   199	We screened six alternative agent designs on the GPT-5.4 calibration split (20 items per domain, 240 items total) before arriving at the router. All uniformly regress the descriptor-enhanced direct baseline: self-refine $-5.9$ pp, naive debate $-7.2$ pp, gated debate $-4.1$ pp, evidence-injected proposer $-10.2$ pp, third-call arbiter $-9.1$ pp, normal-first $-2.4$ pp. The failure mode is consistent: an \emph{unconditional} second VLM call rationalises correct high-confidence predictions away. AnomalyClaw avoids this by (i) using an \emph{external} non-parametric expert as the escalation signal and (ii) using a router rather than a fixed sequence. Full variant table and per-variant failure analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}. These numbers should be read as \emph{design-screening evidence on one backbone's calibration split} rather than cross-backbone generalisation claims.
   200	
   201	\subsection{Complementarity and budget}
   202	\label{ssec:complementarity}
   203	The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.
   204	
   205	\subsection{Limitations}
   206	\label{ssec:limitations}
   207	The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.

