   111	  Rules for this turn:
   112	    - If candidate_features is empty AND initial_score<0.3 → action="final",
   113	      score=0.05, rationale="nothing unusual observed vs refs".
   114	    - Otherwise action="call_tool" with the tool most likely to REFUTE
   115	      the feature at refutation_target — i.e. show it in refs.
   116	      Prefer `tool_side_by_side` when the feature has a specific bbox;
   117	      `tool_reference_profiler` for general "does this count as
   118	      allowed variation" questions; `tool_reference_retriever` when you
   119	      suspect the query belongs to an under-represented normal subtype.
   120	
   121	Turn 2+ — REFUTATION VERDICT:
   122	  After the tool returns, output:
   123	    "refutation_verdict": "found_in_ref"|"not_found"|"inconclusive"
   124	    "feature_status_update": describe what happened to the feature in
   125	      concrete terms (e.g. "the stain pattern appears in ref 2 as well").
   126	    "remaining_candidate_features": updated list with refuted ones removed.
   127	    "updated_score": revised anomaly score based on remaining features.
   128	  Action:
   129	    - If remaining_candidate_features is empty → action="final",
   130	      score=updated_score (should be 0.05-0.20).
   131	    - Else if budget allows → pick next refutation_target and call next tool.
   132	    - Else action="final", score=updated_score.
   133	
   134	DISCIPLINE:
   135	  - NEVER flip your score upward just because a tool "confirms" a feature.
   136	    Tools only give evidence AGAINST your suspicion. A confirmed suspicion
   137	    only prevents down-weighting; it does not boost anomaly.
   138	  - If you call tool_expert_score and rank>=0.85, this increases your prior
